# Keep the Docker build context (and every intermediate layer) free of
# anything that isn't needed to build the production image, per ugcportal-9rt
# acceptance criteria K2 (no dev artifacts / secrets / VCS metadata in the
# final image).
#
# THESE ARE NOT GITIGNORE PATTERNS, and the difference has already bitten
# this file. Docker matches each pattern against the whole path relative to
# the context root and `*` does not cross `/`, so a bare `*.pem` excludes
# `./key.pem` and leaves `certs/key.pem` in the context. Measured on a
# scratch context holding `top.pem` and `certs/nested.pem`: under `*.pem`
# the nested one was still in the built image, under `**/*.pem` neither was
# (ugcportal-fb32). `**/` is what makes a bare name match at every depth,
# the root included.
#
# So the name-based patterns below carry `**/`. The ones left anchored are
# anchored on purpose, for three different reasons:
#   - `.next`, `out`, `build`, `certs`: `**/build` would also drop a source
#     directory called `build`, and the same argument holds for `certs` --
#     `**/certs` would drop a source directory of that name too, not just
#     the one directory this pattern exists to catch. Measured: no tracked
#     directory of any of those four names exists anywhere in this tree
#     except `.next` at the root; `certs/` itself is untracked (gitignored)
#     and is the directory this pattern is protecting against in the first
#     place.
#   - `src/generated`, `.beads/embeddeddolt`, `.beads/proxieddb`,
#     `/coverage`: these name a path, not a name, so `**/` is meaningless.
#   - `.git`, `.gitignore`, `.claude`, `.codex`, `.agents`, `.github`,
#     `.runtime`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`: repo-root
#     tooling and docs. These are NOT depth-proof, and one nested copy does
#     survive into the context -- `.beads/.gitignore`. Left rather than
#     special-cased: it is beads' own ignore rules, not a secret or a dev
#     artifact, so it is outside what K2 is about. The nested AGENTS.md /
#     CLAUDE.md / CHANGELOG.md copies under `.next/standalone/` do not
#     survive, because `.next` is excluded above them.
#   - `docs`, `e2e`, `vitest.config.ts`, `vitest.setup.ts`,
#     `playwright.config.ts`, `eslint.config.mjs`: each exists exactly once,
#     at the repo root (checked), so the same argument as `build`/`certs`
#     applies -- `**/docs` would also drop a source directory of that name.
#     Their own section near the bottom of this file says what each is and
#     why it is not needed in either image.

# Dependencies (reinstalled fresh inside the image; never copy the host's)
**/node_modules
**/npm-debug.log*

# Next.js build output (rebuilt inside the image)
.next
out
build

# Generated Prisma client (regenerated inside the image from
# prisma/schema.prisma - see Dockerfile - so host state never leaks in)
src/generated

# Version control
.git
.gitignore

# Local env files / secrets — never bake these into an image layer.
#
# `**/*.pem` and not `*.pem`: `certs/capgemini.pem` is the corporate-proxy CA
# bundle docker-compose.proxy-ca.yml tells a proxied developer to create, and
# under the bare pattern it reached the build context and BOTH images.
# Measured by rebuilding both with the old pattern (PR #222 round 1):
#   builder  /app/certs/capgemini.pem  and
#            /app/.next/standalone/certs/capgemini.pem   (4334 bytes each)
#   runner   /app/certs/capgemini.pem, owned nextjs:nodejs
# The runner's copy arrived through that standalone directory: Next's
# whole-project tracing sweeps the repo into `.next/standalone`, and the
# runner stage copied that directory wholesale. So a corporate CA bundle was
# being baked into the production image, and -- because git cannot see a
# gitignored file -- two machines produced different image contents under
# one pinned tag. See scripts/build-qa-image.sh for that second half.
#
# The runner stage no longer copies standalone wholesale -- it names what it
# takes (ugcportal-6jcq) -- but the exclusions in this file are still what
# keeps this material out of the BUILDER stage, which `docker-compose.yml`
# runs as the `migrate` service and which copies the whole context. Both
# halves are needed: one file, two images.
**/.env
**/.env*
!.env.example
!env.example
**/*.pem
# ...and the directory docker-compose.proxy-ca.yml tells you to put the
# bundle in, by name as well: PROXY_CA_FILE accepts any path, but a bundle
# exported as `.crt`/`.cer` into `certs/` would slip past `**/*.pem`. Listing
# the directory also stops `COPY . .` leaving an empty /app/certs husk in the
# builder stage (checked both ways).
certs

# Beads issue tracker local database (large, irrelevant to the app, and
# never something that should end up in an image)
.beads/embeddeddolt
.beads/proxieddb

# Editor / OS cruft
**/.DS_Store

# Agent tooling / docs not needed at build or runtime
.claude
.codex
.agents
.github
.runtime
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
CHANGELOG.md

# Test artifacts
/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Tests and the dev tooling that runs them. Nothing here is read by `npm ci`,
# `prisma generate` or `next build` -- checked by building the image with all
# of it excluded -- and nothing here is read by the server at run time, so it
# belongs in neither image. This is the pattern that used to put 258
# *.test.*/*.spec.* files in the production image (ugcportal-6jcq).
#
# `**/` on the two name patterns because test files sit at every depth of
# src/; the four config files are anchored because each exists exactly once,
# at the repo root, and `**/vitest.config.ts` would say otherwise. Not
# depth-proof and deliberately so, the same reasoning as `.git`/`CLAUDE.md`
# above.
#
# `*.test.*` and not `*.test*`: `src/lib/legal/legal-page.test-support.ts`
# and `src/lib/test-support/` are imported BY tests rather than being tests,
# and the dot keeps them out of this -- which is moot for the runner, where
# the Dockerfile's allowlist excludes all of src/ anyway, and not moot for
# the builder, where a pattern that swallowed them would break nothing until
# someone ran the suite in a container.
**/*.test.*
**/*.spec.*
vitest.config.ts
vitest.setup.ts
playwright.config.ts
eslint.config.mjs

# Documentation and end-to-end specs. Neither is read at build or run time
# (the only references to `docs/` from src/ are prose in comments and
# rendered pointer strings, not file reads -- src/lib/design/tokens.ts reads
# src/app/globals.css, not docs/design/tokens.css).
#
# docs/ is the sharpest thing the old wholesale copy shipped, which is why it
# is excluded here rather than left to the runner's allowlist alone:
# `docs/legal/manual-upload-rights-review.md` exists to enumerate where the
# rights gate is weak and `docs/legal/instagram-resale-rights-checklist.md`
# records which uploaders cannot be cleared. A map of the legal controls'
# soft spots does not belong in a deployed artifact of any stage.
#
# Anchored, not `**/docs`: each exists exactly once, at the repo root
# (checked), and `**/docs` would also drop a source directory that happened
# to be called `docs` -- the same argument the header makes for `build` and
# `certs`.
docs
e2e

# Local SQLite dev databases
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
